# /etc/tmpfiles.d/vms.conf — the directories the VMS's units mount, made at every boot whoever starts first.
# Installed and applied by install-obsd.sh (`systemd-tmpfiles --create`), after `w2c.conf` (the platform's: its stores,
# its secrets, the console's door — `w2c.tmpfiles`).
# ================================================================================================
# NOTES — what every part of this file does and why (kept beside the code, not in a separate document)
# ================================================================================================
# # vms.tmpfiles — /run/vms, /run/vms-obsd, /data/vms and the box's own volume
#
# **Role.** A container's `Volume=` of a host directory that does not exist does not start. /run is a tmpfs, empty
# at every boot, and /run/vms was made only by obsd.service (then `RuntimeDirectory=obsd vms`): without the daemon —
# not installed yet, or failing — neither `vmsworker@` nor `recworker@` started, and the box held no camera at all
# for want of an archive engine (the review's fourth pass, blocker 2). So the directories are the box's, made here:
#
# - `/run/vms` — the workers' shared-memory branches (`live_shm`), written by the holder and read by a recorder on
#   this box; both run as root in their containers.
# - `/run/vms-obsd` — the daemon's socket, 0750 `vms-obsd:vms-obsd`: only the daemon and its clients' group get in.
#   The unit's own `RuntimeDirectory=vms-obsd` makes the same directory when the daemon starts; this one is for a
#   recorder that starts first, whose mount of it would otherwise fail. The product's path (its `vms-obsd.service`);
#   the course's was /run/obsd.
# - `/data/vms` — the VMS's own on the data partition (the platform's is `/data/platform`, `w2c.tmpfiles`): `etc/`
#   is this box's `/etc/vms` (`vms.env`; the link is laid by `install-obsd.sh`), `obsd/` the archive engine's volumes.
# - `/data/vms/obsd/volume` — the box's own volume, where a recorder with nothing declared writes (`ARCHIVE_VOLUME`,
#   by default `vms/config.py`'s `OWN_VOLUME`). obsd is the VMS's (the owner, 4 October), so its volumes are under
#   `/data/vms/obsd`, and never inside the platform's events archive. The daemon opens it, as `vms-obsd`, so
#   `vms-obsd` owns it. On the data partition, never the rootfs slot (М9 Lesson 5): an OS update must not take the
#   archive with it — which is why it is not the product's `StateDirectory=` (/var/lib, on the slot). A volume
#   declared on another path of this box is chowned by `install-obsd.sh <path>`. `d` sets the directory's owner and
#   mode only; a ring formatted when the daemon ran as root is moved over once, by the install script, not at every
#   boot — and a box whose ring is still at the old `/data/volume` keeps it there (`install-obsd.sh` says so in
#   `vms.env`).
# ================================================================================================
d /run/vms         0755 root root    -
d /run/vms-obsd    0750 vms-obsd vms-obsd -
d /data/vms              0755 root     root     -
d /data/vms/etc          0755 root     root     -
d /data/vms/obsd         0750 vms-obsd vms-obsd -
d /data/vms/obsd/volume  0750 vms-obsd vms-obsd -
